  "clock": {
    "mode": "narration",
    "lines": [
      {
        "id": "v01",
        "text": "On the darkest night of Kartik, India lights a row of lamps. This is Diwali, the festival of lights.",
        "start": 0,
        "duration": 6.1
      },
      {
        "id": "v02",
        "text": "Why Lakshmi? She is the goddess of prosperity and abundance. Her lotus rises from muddy water and still opens clean.",
        "start": 6.7,
        "duration": 7,
        "gainDb": -19
      },
      {
        "id": "v03",
        "text": "One beloved story begins in the churning of the cosmic ocean, when gods and asuras sought the nectar of immortality.",
        "start": 14.2,
        "duration": 6.7
      },
      {
        "id": "v04",
        "text": "Many treasures rose. Then Lakshmi appeared on a lotus, radiant, lotuses in her hands.",
        "start": 21.6,
        "duration": 5.1
      },
      {
        "id": "v05",
        "text": "She chose Vishnu. Some call Diwali her birthday; others, her wedding.",
        "start": 26.7,
        "duration": 4.4,
        "gainDb": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "v06",
        "text": "So homes are cleaned, rangoli drawn at the door, windows left open, diyas lit, a path for her.",
        "start": 31.8,
        "duration": 5.7
      },
      {
        "id": "v07",
        "text": "Lakshmi Puja asks for prosperity. The old stories add a condition: wealth stays where there is effort, virtue and generosity.",
        "start": 38,
        "duration": 7.5
      },
      {
        "id": "v08",
        "text": "In Bengal the same night is Kali's; in the north, Rama's return; in the west, new account books open.",
        "start": 46.3,
        "duration": 6.2
      },
      {
        "id": "v09",
        "text": "Light a lamp. Make room. Share what you have. That is how the light comes in, and stays.",
        "start": 53,
        "duration": 4.5
      }
    ]
  },
